    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 200', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 200\\n neighbor 5.5.5.5 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: This assumes that:\\n1. The BGP AS is 200 (as indicated by the group name \"remote-as-200\")\\n2. The IP address 4.4.4.4 is configured on Loopback0 interface in the Cisco configuration\\n3. The neighbor IP matches the one in the original Juniper configuration (5.5.5.5)\\n4. The \"update-source\" command in Cisco serves the same purpose as \"local-address\" in Juniper for specifying the source IP for BGP sessions', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 200'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 5.5.5.5 update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 200\\n neighbor 5.5.5.5 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: This assumes that:\\n1. The BGP AS is 200 (as indicated by the group name \"remote-as-200\")\\n2. The IP address 4.4.4.4 is configured on Loopback0 interface in the Cisco configuration\\n3. The neighbor IP matches the one in the original Juniper configuration (5.5.5.5)\\n4. The \"update-source\" command in Cisco serves the same purpose as \"local-address\" in Juniper for specifying the source IP for BGP sessions', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 5.5.5.5 update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['export [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'}]]": 2,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.3.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The BGP process is initiated with the AS number (100 in this case)\\n2. The neighbor command specifies both the neighbor IP and its AS number\\n3. Unlike Juniper's group-based configuration, Cisco uses individual neighbor statements under the BGP process\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.3.1.1 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.3.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The BGP process is initiated with the AS number (100 in this case)\\n2. The neighbor command specifies both the neighbor IP and its AS number\\n3. Unlike Juniper's group-based configuration, Cisco uses individual neighbor statements under the BGP process\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.3.1.1 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.3.1.1 update-source 10.3.1.2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, BGP local address is specified using the `update-source` parameter under the neighbor configuration\\n- The command maintains the same neighbor IP (10.3.1.1) and local address (10.3.1.2) as in the Juniper configuration\\n- The AS number (100) is specified in the `router bgp` context in Cisco, rather than in the group name like Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.3.1.1 update-source 10.3.1.2', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.3.1.1 update-source 10.3.1.2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, BGP local address is specified using the `update-source` parameter under the neighbor configuration\\n- The command maintains the same neighbor IP (10.3.1.1) and local address (10.3.1.2) as in the Juniper configuration\\n- The AS number (100) is specified in the `router bgp` context in Cisco, rather than in the group name like Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.3.1.1 update-source 10.3.1.2'}]]": 1,
